#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124285 :question/id #uuid "7c28a136-0b86-5d13-a5f8-b0dd0e0ea1ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-3963" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=|x-2|-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x \\in (-\\infty, 2]$\n\n- $x \\in (0, \\infty)$\n\n- $x \\in (-1,4]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's graph the given function. The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-2|-2$ can be obtained in the following way:  \n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right,  to get $y= |x-2|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y= |x-2|$ by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=|x-2|-2.$\n\n![](images/60/60c2601abfd3c4034991408f47b75c8cf70a1bccb72f73b2f9624a52d0ed7b42.png)\n\nNow, remember that a function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in(-\\infty,2].$  Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/45/45b0cdd26280d9379a449af41edbc9e69dd9d76f3b4116d938fbe708c21f6f29.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in(0,\\infty)$ and $x \\in (-1,4].$ On these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/55/55b8483c59362d3a59dfd269db74f2c5f17a97cee1f25c896ece16890d80bd42.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b27a71-8627-553d-b3b7-736e498a4b2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333054} {:answer/id #uuid "094b9895-e6b6-5c84-904b-7748f61b2bd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333075} {:answer/id #uuid "292d8888-725c-5063-9b6e-8a489b08f92b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333369} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8d7775-ce84-5e09-b7f9-dc721abba388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333994} {:answer/id #uuid "8496717f-5bba-5751-8934-d2a8aeb6efb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "292d8888-725c-5063-9b6e-8a489b08f92b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333369} :answer-field/id #uuid "257150c1-6d20-56ba-86c5-f7814a686dee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335309}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ea11e93-5fca-5cd0-afe6-c972b9a2cce3" :question/math-academy-id "q-66482" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}+2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-2,2)$\n- $x\\in [0,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,0]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units up to get $f(x)={x}^{2}+2.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff3d1ac7500d83574b8671b23e99d4a0f1dbd6ab9cebc906aa06ff2c21f95345.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,2).$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/a7/a71ce036adc5dc0b15fce9e367a0c8512699dc6a7814f4e9a1886696d2abad6e.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the intervals $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and  $x\\in (-\\infty ,0].$ On these intervals, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/68/6858bd1fab8d9659c84fa941cd63ee9f509c8364dce0e753706c640ecfe6e9ec.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23d0843d-f9d9-53fc-af2f-70a86972fbce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333318} {:answer/id #uuid "412a7385-2c06-5d4c-ac37-0f84026f3a76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333603} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb699d0-cd94-5a03-a574-8917a2ac4e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334007} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d97bcc-489c-515d-98cd-9ee8a1c3da76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334990} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c9284a-dd6b-5244-871b-d2e03d20fb5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fb699d0-cd94-5a03-a574-8917a2ac4e97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334007} :answer-field/id #uuid "0040f0de-6a5e-51de-b8b9-d2321e25e753" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335238}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c08528cd-5efd-5572-9af4-e4dc31c682da" :question/math-academy-id "q-66723" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=x+1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-1,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-10,5)$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $1$ and $y$-intercept $1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/00/00d6394343d441ee10fa20ee029b8f89b52ff1e6ded8e7fdbe0756118212f1a8.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (-1,\\infty )$ and $x\\in (-10,5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234209 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "912ff428-058e-5197-af7a-fcbe7f3d6387" :question/math-academy-id "q-66785" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=|x-4|+2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. - $x\\in (-\\infty ,3)$\nII. - $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\nIII. - $x\\in (-\\infty ,5]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-4|+2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $4$ units to the right,  to get $y=|x-4|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=|x-4|$ by $2$ units up to get $f(x)=|x-4|+2.$\n\n![](images/56/56956d0f23ae6828098507712fd00525dc4f114a195716dc16e63d8475e9c143.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,3).$  Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in (1,\\infty )$  and $(-\\infty ,5].$ On each of these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323741 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa590220-f3a0-5655-826b-3efab531bea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322487} {:answer/id #uuid "d0287869-916d-5063-b126-5876daa4c5ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322772} {:answer/id #uuid "df32af73-fc23-5cf6-aef3-aef4290f85f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322893} {:answer/id #uuid "ecc19260-952f-5ed7-b689-d9003b659719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323014} {:answer/id #uuid "edcdd0a3-12d3-570f-bd72-a7840745eedb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0287869-916d-5063-b126-5876daa4c5ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322772} :answer-field/id #uuid "acd1f6e7-a3d9-5ab4-bf8e-1a83738037a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323505}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aef31e6b-2918-5842-b5c4-348781827064" :question/math-academy-id "q-36010" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=|x-2|+1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$\n- $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (2,4]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-2|+1$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right,  to get $y=|x-2|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=|x-2|$ by $1$ unit up to get $f(x)=|x-2|+1.$\n\n![](images/ce/ceab1637313ae53869cc1e59f6a56ad6cb920a83dffba482558b696188df0d36.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$ and $x\\in (2,4].$ On these intervals, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/a4/a47169838785b6f4ac78253252bcedc3c2cb752a655feac9945e20dd40563b4f.png)\n\n  \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/14/14ed35294e1179ffd9efaf87fa8ac1eb99853a7da344e462c2cb0dda74c3b78f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c92f901-9b2a-5dd0-a311-5f44155934d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333561} {:answer/id #uuid "6efe0f16-e6b6-5943-b170-b88d45f78989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334000} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2f54b5-a442-5111-91a7-4f8042c17ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334116} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e9067e-96b6-5e89-980e-2099570a92ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334428} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0e7650-c97f-5547-8404-3506e873448e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e2f54b5-a442-5111-91a7-4f8042c17ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334116} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ba0ff0c-c72a-5014-bd52-d93eabbfcd56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335449}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2712841f-ac1b-5d69-a817-019e60c121da" :question/math-academy-id "q-66483" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=1-x,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (0,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-2,2)$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $-1$ and $y$-intercept $1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/73/73d9277c040ae457ba6d96e162574edbeeef965d181fa6011ca4ab0e4644bd82.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ and $x\\in (-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335893 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057e6ce9-267d-57cc-ae69-ad84b7ee42ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333035} {:answer/id #uuid "2670d583-ba23-509d-984a-336387ded173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333342} {:answer/id #uuid "818226b6-2143-5b12-bb7a-35ba69eb59b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334139} {:answer/id #uuid "81f500aa-4f70-5107-844f-19fa4e38c1f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334145} {:answer/id #uuid "9c018dec-8ad1-56e9-99a1-bc03b2303eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c018dec-8ad1-56e9-99a1-bc03b2303eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334363} :answer-field/id #uuid "66899f0d-563e-5ed9-a6cb-77e4a5b3f25b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335438}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26ae354c-e4a4-50d9-b820-8ba146650b10" :question/math-academy-id "q-66669" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(x-2)^{2},$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (2,\\infty )$\n\nII. $x\\in (-1,5)$\n\nIII. $x\\in (-\\infty ,2]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=(x-2)^{2},$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n1\\. Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it by $2$ units  to  the right  $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1e3ac550e35a3dc93b3e26e5834f763847b04f5133de02e368124f54dd2b9dd6.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-1,5).$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/3e/3ebb316ca122b6786b8ef501fc7c56f8e0e68c8cfc32e5801c493d38382fc26c.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (2,\\infty )$  and $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$  On these intervals, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/86/86c7c9cacc7a3868565f33130563ffed0ca423aa087e76cc08d103ea171822ed.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17c1e9df-cdaa-5e98-bb5a-91b82a10bb92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333202} {:answer/id #uuid "253331ca-df9d-523a-8811-2487a95d3272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333334} {:answer/id #uuid "63786589-d496-56b9-bccb-1a7b14aff44f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333890} {:answer/id #uuid "9bfaecb8-b49e-5068-aef0-3b70aa437c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334362} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2e9670-55bb-542c-a3ec-ea63c48da4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17c1e9df-cdaa-5e98-bb5a-91b82a10bb92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333202} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bd33408-f473-5e92-b602-88c8f1b4211b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335418}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffb9d3e2-6b9a-5fb3-b64e-0b7d921759d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-66679" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$\n\nII. $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\n\nIII. $x\\in (-1,3]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n1\\. Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it by $1$ units to the right,  to get $y=(x-1)^{2}.$\n\n3\\. Finally, translate $y=(x-1)^{2}$  by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2.$\n\n![](images/f5/f53a4f97166877085c90acc01e3f8d0c6ac7bb3f704bbfb792d747122d314013.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$ and $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ On these intervals, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/b0/b088c8ed1974a7c259dd54f85aec7a124579981971babfe8dee0313ca23b6458.png)\n\n  \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-1,3].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/b3/b3af13782a01213bb09fdeef38fbeb3fbca3f41348036e6786b4efa74a0166b4.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "289033af-746b-57e4-8f86-92f6034dddbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333366} {:answer/id #uuid "5a0ca728-76b0-59d2-8cf2-38f34d12ab44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333812} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ff2b4e-17d4-5465-aab7-ee5598712ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334542} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a3233a-13a0-5b0d-bedc-05bdebf0a689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334587} {:answer/id #uuid "c1cbbd57-bce5-5d75-b881-127cf8bac14a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a0ca728-76b0-59d2-8cf2-38f34d12ab44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333812} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c83ae85-26c4-5f9f-9820-b59a10f14cd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335451}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1120070d-b929-5ac5-a6ba-244a40b57b70" :question/math-academy-id "q-66687" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,0]$\n- $x\\in (-2,2]$\n- $x\\in (-2,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+1$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Reflect its graph across  the $x$-axis to get $y=-{x}^{2}.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=-{x}^{2}$  by $1$ unit up to  get $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+1.$\n\n![](images/83/83a9f5966c76b89b2cad9c6244e2e447fb0052fc7f946df954df76265628c4a7.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n*The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,0].$ On this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/f6/f694b6b1bc979289b748758385e05575990bd866457e75a84fbf7bdf335c03b9.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,2].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once. Since  $(-2,2]$ is a subset of $(-2,\\infty )$, our function is not invertible on $x\\in (-2,\\infty )$ either.\n\n![](images/c0/c03118f3fd0193910b49e26ab5ea56e4320fd23583feb3267cd4442d0a157583.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d39eb8e-2acf-5989-93c9-5706e83e7f56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334106} {:answer/id #uuid "86eabbba-0a20-58a9-b168-1143669ceead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334184} {:answer/id #uuid "e1269f37-8751-57fd-a7c9-4c6a8b91e67d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334948} {:answer/id #uuid "e3884308-b6b1-5abc-b3d7-146aec3aae9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334972} {:answer/id #uuid "e693a8f1-2c29-5ee8-9bb7-6a65568289ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3884308-b6b1-5abc-b3d7-146aec3aae9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334972} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0d2f2b9-b380-5289-b8eb-9732e9ebb27b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335539}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02e022db-b323-5e31-af64-39f79b6a6465" :question/math-academy-id "q-66725" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=-x+3,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\nII. $x\\in (0.5,\\infty )$\n\nIII. $x\\in [-3,\\sqrt{2}]$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $-1$ and $y$-intercept $3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/aa/aaa05d1ab0fd630dacd81723fe00472cb901fb6f73af5f111f44459476191c0f.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (0.5,\\infty )$ and $x\\in [-3,\\sqrt{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09071e15-b470-5b7e-9b11-54f49cdedf1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333072} {:answer/id #uuid "2c5ccd7d-3b34-57fa-9756-01a55bf6d389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333408} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4a0865-5f57-5aac-9e60-dbe69aaa7c32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333415} {:answer/id #uuid "db70806a-1a03-5f87-9afd-163b0c228e5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334908} {:answer/id #uuid "fce2b562-b937-5bdf-944f-df98ec166dbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09071e15-b470-5b7e-9b11-54f49cdedf1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333072} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5f3f896-e079-54b5-988f-9af076e4c242" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335669}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ee60b09-63e9-5076-abae-7337fc233de5" :question/math-academy-id "q-66729" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=|x-1|-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$\n\nII. $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\n\nIII. $x\\in (-\\infty ,2]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-1|-2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n1\\. Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n2\\. Translate it by $1$ units to the right,  to get $y=|x-1|.$\n\n3\\. Finally, translate $y=|x-1|$ by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=|x-1|-2.$\n\n![](images/75/753840da17642582f9dbc0debc18f1fa3efcbbb11142894c1dd823ad6c0c630a.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$ and $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ On these intervals, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/15/15f1c797cbddd2ac8e2e9c5be72f842e7d1accfc5f619af57b1f35a27e1015c0.png)\n\n  \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/a1/a100489b49da7b06c98783b1b1c4ec0de9d891fc2b22f4b36b1a0ac2d54fc4f3.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7a8b58-513e-5c23-befb-bf2a0c2d6677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333084} {:answer/id #uuid "40353dfd-44f9-5911-a718-77d6c856581c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333596} {:answer/id #uuid "680fefff-b2a2-53dd-9095-2495312a2ec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333944} {:answer/id #uuid "c5946377-6c26-50bf-b6cd-617222e6c9d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334705} {:answer/id #uuid "d517b26b-3f62-59f6-936d-776240726217" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "680fefff-b2a2-53dd-9095-2495312a2ec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333944} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b0a3ce3-3f93-5d1d-a683-088b44a950bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335388}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7aa0547e-3ef1-575a-ba36-cf93ffa1c8dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-66778" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=|x+1|-3,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$\n- $x\\in [1,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x+1|-3$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $1$ units to the left,  to get $y=|x+1|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=|x+1|$ by $3$ units down to get $f(x)=|x+1|-3.$\n\n![](images/3b/3b9f56280089991c7db7652d84a777edd5c5e012921f0d48362f4fc3e4fd2a6e.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]$ and $x\\in [1,\\infty ).$ On these intervals, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/fd/fd0b0fe70e009f43e80b0f712b6cfd576deb4c4c0457f61adae04cf7e44f140c.png)\n\n  \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,1].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/58/582ec1960b053731bf201c84d8c58fd4e1734a2b085cd5549a01aace4b600b4a.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d49c863-bcca-5003-b60b-2402ac1bbc16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396689} {:answer/id #uuid "372a1527-fcb7-5e97-9214-ceab892980ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396690} {:answer/id #uuid "89d11ef2-0ebb-56ef-85f5-eff24a1c20ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396691} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8bd46d-8040-58fd-9418-5e14e16fd9fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396692} {:answer/id #uuid "3ecd9f23-0f52-529d-9d3d-4b581fbbb61a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e8bd46d-8040-58fd-9418-5e14e16fd9fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396692} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb55a4d3-ef9d-5ba1-bc8d-64b5b8502eb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396688}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8061fba9-94fb-59ab-bb67-c981e34d25e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-66724" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2x+4,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\nII. $x\\in (10,\\infty )$\nIII. $x\\in [-5,5)$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $2$ and $y$-intercept $4,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/d3/d33314230419ec762bc4ad8603b2e6c3436461cd6b4b6fe4783d12a23d01b660.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (10,\\infty )$ and $x\\in [-5,5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124569 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124378 :question/id #uuid "ff1853b3-627e-5c73-be98-b1d9a586f198" :question/math-academy-id "e-7020" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf{F},$ where $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2xy\\ \\mathbf i + (x^2 - 3)\\ \\mathbf j .$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}\\,\\textbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}}\\,\\textbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2-3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 + g'(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 -3$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & ={x}^{2}-3 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =-3 \\\\ g(y) & =-3y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=-3y +C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2y -3y + C.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97730a2e-3d3e-5a2c-b366-7a3b1eafbe63" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47df7b6d-fec9-535f-8b88-a117b8bfa970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=-xy-y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321712} {:answer/id #uuid "5561e91a-bebe-5e3f-bcd9-eb69c2455d3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3-xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321822} {:answer/id #uuid "67bdc89f-375e-56ea-8ea4-a17278d434c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=-xy+2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321957} {:answer/id #uuid "735f2dc9-74b2-5218-b349-1e5f8f79f77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=-xy+3y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322048} {:answer/id #uuid "b67836fc-421d-5b22-b35b-660c1238530c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\frac{y{x}^{2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "735f2dc9-74b2-5218-b349-1e5f8f79f77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=-xy+3y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322048} :answer-field/id #uuid "f868516f-34ef-5985-b18a-ffbd80bfb8cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323655}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c542ab02-5931-58f2-9193-271289d6b23c" :question/math-academy-id "q-35994" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=-y\\,i+(3-x)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3-x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (-y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-xy+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3-x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+{g}^{′}(y) & =3-x \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =3 \\\\ g(y) & =3y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=3y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=-xy+3y+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be96c265-1017-528e-b2d6-bd980d7b8fcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) = \\sin x - \\cos y + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122967} {:answer/id #uuid "c0b0cf71-b5d4-5ffa-992c-b6eae6169fb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) = 2\\sin x - \\cos y + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122968} {:answer/id #uuid "fe4ff845-bbcb-5b11-bcf6-43630a40f33c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) = 3\\sin x - 2\\cos x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122969} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7e77a0-486c-5a56-b533-08bc37e948f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) = \\sin x + \\cos y + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122970} {:answer/id #uuid "389955b0-7237-58eb-b596-06025a5afdb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) = 2\\sin x + 3\\cos y + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122971}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c764614f-427c-5917-aeca-aca7cb940472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122966}] :question/id #uuid "6c610779-afb5-50f5-812b-1837ea05cfe1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37346" :question/problem "Find $f(x, y)$ such that $∇f(x, y) = F(x, y)$, where $F(x, y) = \\cos xi + \\sin yj$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235530 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb910bef-90e1-5421-8635-db1d4004f016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{3}-x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb910bef-90e1-5421-8635-db1d4004f016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{3}-x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234919} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f37f7d6-7efc-58de-84cc-2e728fce1077" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235001}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4340b95-6cd1-5844-9535-9e98d780f1e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-168602" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x,y)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+\\,C,$\nwhere $C$ is a constant-" :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that $a=1.$ So, \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=({y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j.\n$$\n\nThe gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{3}-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({y}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{y}^{3}-x+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & =3x{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =1 \\\\ g(y) & =y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-x+y+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc64aee-0612-5f70-8c72-8df1a1999481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263274} {:answer/id #uuid "66ba7e97-e4b3-5011-86a1-36eb79dfb963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263889} {:answer/id #uuid "6912f021-7da7-5c61-90c4-999143bf1452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263904} {:answer/id #uuid "ca52d8a2-ba6f-54b5-bcce-c59555ea401a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+x+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264768} {:answer/id #uuid "e230c0a5-63e0-55e5-b44e-b91248e79909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66ba7e97-e4b3-5011-86a1-36eb79dfb963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263889} :answer-field/id #uuid "757d31c0-99cc-5826-a461-7d448963c375" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265448}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57b858a9-b536-522e-9275-67da69f513d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-132077" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+xy+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ g(y) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+xy+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06efc43d-eb38-5b98-874e-9f55c0bbbdba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263062} {:answer/id #uuid "4810273b-53be-5106-85a6-d821e49d030b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263623} {:answer/id #uuid "647221ea-f3e1-5a39-9529-f097c2e5c0a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=y{e}^{y}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263870} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2c2b5a-de03-5ff6-a222-2914886eb6cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{y}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263952} {:answer/id #uuid "e825a2d0-6272-5c4f-9792-c5bc1bfd370a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e825a2d0-6272-5c4f-9792-c5bc1bfd370a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265020} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf944f96-ad7d-5769-a11c-26d605688403" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265580}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7b637df-9182-5d4f-997d-44163c023403" :question/math-academy-id "q-132079" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=({e}^{y}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{y}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({e}^{y}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{g}^{′}(y) & =x{e}^{y}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ g(y) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff13513-2c62-551f-b51b-384eba917a5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=2y{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263146} {:answer/id #uuid "496b60d9-0d25-59bd-9c19-6467809cfe4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263632} {:answer/id #uuid "63500018-57ff-5df1-bcb6-85380b2c7d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263858} {:answer/id #uuid "6c452c58-6b42-5c49-8585-0f39c63c429b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=y{e}^{y}+x{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263933} {:answer/id #uuid "affded30-c6e0-5d42-b054-9af00e3bf815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "496b60d9-0d25-59bd-9c19-6467809cfe4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263632} :answer-field/id #uuid "944f1edc-f0d0-51d2-983f-32b6ee33f7f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265513}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74939473-4e44-5a23-a29d-cf0a790a9021" :question/math-academy-id "q-132080" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+2y)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+2y\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{e}^{y}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+2y$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}+{g}^{′}(y) & =x{e}^{y}+2y \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =2y \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{2}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32cd3a46-2070-5c04-b850-8162eeb789a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348898} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d8f137-d9d9-5d7a-bf57-3e496c94821f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350047} {:answer/id #uuid "cd48e39f-f120-599e-9a6b-494a2b1af79a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350251} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d34f2d-177c-57dd-a130-fa54336633eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350468} {:answer/id #uuid "ed148d5a-2003-5ebe-a806-ce910794f76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=4xy+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6d34f2d-177c-57dd-a130-fa54336633eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350468} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c564c77-be41-5cc9-9524-b2c24202e626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350787}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3063cdc9-a985-5db1-8c3b-1970a3def78b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81734" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=(2x{y}^{2}+1)\\,i+(2{x}^{2}y+1)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2x{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}y+{g}^{′}(y) & =2{x}^{2}y+1 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =1 \\\\ g(y) & =y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19bc219a-35b4-506a-8788-1ce7920044b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348662} {:answer/id #uuid "697a9c9c-4964-5cc5-b1e1-e9e4ce855bc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349423} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d3d365-e63f-5485-9f7c-bb976d8dbf45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=12{x}^{4}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}-\\frac{x}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350095} {:answer/id #uuid "dfcbf686-54eb-5d98-ad26-4a8fcc350136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{1}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350408} {:answer/id #uuid "e346b178-9934-5d6b-b8a5-f42de5668c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=12{x}^{3}y-\\frac{x}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfcbf686-54eb-5d98-ad26-4a8fcc350136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{1}{y}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350408} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2cd2466-47dd-5469-8369-868df3afb4d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350984}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cb4b31d-3aca-59fb-b7c0-b708932a190d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81737" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ where $F(x,y)=(12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,i+(\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}})\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ g(y) & =\\frac{1}{y}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=\\frac{1}{y}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3{x}^{4}-\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{1}{y}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d51cd1-8285-536a-8208-7ac9db192459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348483} {:answer/id #uuid "3248783c-f3e7-502c-98ea-2421859e9686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={sin}^{2}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348894} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb98351-4331-5e63-877c-5e62c1993a42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349291} {:answer/id #uuid "dd07eefc-581e-52c8-9b46-f43da8da7576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350377} {:answer/id #uuid "edbfac28-c489-5133-bf62-26d082cbf2e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bb98351-4331-5e63-877c-5e62c1993a42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349291} :answer-field/id #uuid "d462b30f-3414-577e-a159-9545494e03c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351075}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "988a8975-0a4f-5e3c-8020-c74ebb3ac42c" :question/math-academy-id "q-81748" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ g(y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "103b348f-34f3-5535-a8f9-7d28169b37c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348588} {:answer/id #uuid "7f84441d-8ba3-55dc-9186-bdf8e2359017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=xy{e}^{xy}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349618} {:answer/id #uuid "a56f077a-269d-5153-8c08-67abd9324a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}+2+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349921} {:answer/id #uuid "b19e7881-c9e2-5039-a363-ad998058af6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={e}^{xy}+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350016} {:answer/id #uuid "bf984817-303a-595b-bcf3-132f39ff1a10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={e}^{xy}+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b19e7881-c9e2-5039-a363-ad998058af6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={e}^{xy}+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350016} :answer-field/id #uuid "76fbc979-ace6-5bac-be82-3f41abff1151" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350912}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb698ef5-cb64-5f1c-8b17-ff6e160a3a79" :question/math-academy-id "q-81749" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ where $F(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\text{ }i+(x{e}^{xy}+2y)\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}+2y\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}+2y$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{xy}+{g}^{′}(y) & =x{e}^{xy}+2y \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =2y \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{2}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{xy}+{y}^{2}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dea30ea-6d79-5529-9cdd-2d23960e3a15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406662} {:answer/id #uuid "1bfaa94c-d375-5358-a83c-08ec5ee1ffba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406664} {:answer/id #uuid "1e611253-3d99-509d-a228-1da84f8f823e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+2y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406665} {:answer/id #uuid "3f756955-ff37-5042-8335-ab5bb657b76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406671} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3780f0-041a-52d5-a729-947e413446bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dea30ea-6d79-5529-9cdd-2d23960e3a15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406662} :answer-field/id #uuid "32c4c17f-d3a1-54b7-85ad-332eeea0c340" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406690}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98240cfd-67f4-53e3-a23f-45405606e2e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-132084" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2})\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{g}^{′}(y) & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2} \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{3}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{y}^{3}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03fb1154-e9d5-5abf-a426-4de833dd30db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406661} {:answer/id #uuid "30f1d918-8f71-5b48-b248-54d76cf6d20c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406669} {:answer/id #uuid "3e98411d-fb1f-5835-98ed-95631d5c3272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2}y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406670} {:answer/id #uuid "92d03502-0368-5753-b847-438a0c827b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406680} {:answer/id #uuid "e73fdb1a-1b58-5eba-ad8a-569d465a93c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03fb1154-e9d5-5abf-a426-4de833dd30db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406661} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a04f87b-0a35-5b54-b59b-82f029326452" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406692}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3be742e-e07b-5a79-9e12-1ef7df6cb24a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81736" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ where $F(x,y)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y)\\text{ }i+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x)\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x+{g}^{′}(y) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ g(y) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ea3ea19-4e92-5abd-88c9-1066a9bed13d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406666} {:answer/id #uuid "507422e5-32ae-5d72-ab98-c3033c109f1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+3y+{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406674} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb42bf5-1fac-5e8e-8246-de98ef76481f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+3{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406677} {:answer/id #uuid "83195e93-81c4-5d1c-886b-c248874b3080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406679} {:answer/id #uuid "adcd9639-c75a-551b-9cba-97a7274611c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ea3ea19-4e92-5abd-88c9-1066a9bed13d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406666} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4e00321-0365-5097-9e12-24d700cbbfb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406694}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6e8edea-70b0-524c-abbc-330734c9a85f" :question/math-academy-id "q-37343" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=2xy\\,i+({x}^{2}+3{y}^{2})\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the functoin $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & ={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{3}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b6c4808-c2a7-5c97-8912-c4f3de3ef57d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}+4y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421418} {:answer/id #uuid "7c064342-1507-5fe3-91dc-0ff15726350b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{3}-{y}^{3}+4y-x{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421419} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebe7055-048b-506e-b0e8-41cb887f2884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{3}+4y-x{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421420} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8717c4-00ef-5595-aeca-97e8db51eb3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}+4y-x{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421421} {:answer/id #uuid "c86c75a8-56c7-544d-9bc9-6d9a38de128c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}+4y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b6c4808-c2a7-5c97-8912-c4f3de3ef57d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}+4y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421418} :answer-field/id #uuid "23f524e3-bd79-5da0-98c3-54cfe15b71e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421423}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49bf02f5-63a7-5afa-b61f-cb52dc9f8048" :question/math-academy-id "q-81738" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ where $F(x,y)=(3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(4-2xy)\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4-2xy\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2xy+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4-2xy$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2xy+{g}^{′}(y) & =4-2xy \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =4 \\\\ g(y) & =4y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=4y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}+4y+C.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Two-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124574 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124382 :question/id #uuid "09d12c46-4ac8-53d9-80a6-ca68f98e0c5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7184" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)  = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = yz\\,\\mathbf{i} + (xz + 4z^2)\\,\\mathbf{j} + (xy+8yz)\\,\\mathbf{k}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} \\, \\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)  = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = yz, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int yz\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =xyz+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + \\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xz+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =xz+4{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =4{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z) = 4yz^2 + h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x, y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy + 8yz + h'(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy+8yz & =xy+8yz+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + C.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8219c68e-f3f4-56a9-8dee-80eaf456059c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y, z) = x^{2}y + 2yz + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122745} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee2dd6e-a063-5ce6-ba25-1e01e48c63c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y, z) = x^{2}y - 3yz + Cz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122746} {:answer/id #uuid "c8899973-d846-566d-aff8-1ee753154df7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y, z) = xy^{2} + 2yz + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122747} {:answer/id #uuid "2d271ef1-cc52-51d9-8679-408b178e46be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y, z) = x^{2}yz + yz^{2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122748} {:answer/id #uuid "5833e6fd-e2cb-5015-9115-e437b9926197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y, z) = x^{2}y + yz + Cxz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122749}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d2a39a8c-dc65-5667-9694-693d1c689df2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122744}] :question/id #uuid "b00bfeb0-ca4e-5b6e-8b0c-afb0a62e4170" :question/math-academy-id "q-36977" :question/problem "Find $f(x, y, z)$ such that $∇f(x, y, z) = F(x, y, z)$, where $F(x, y, z) = 2xyi + (x^{2} + 2z)j + 2yk$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03292879-7e19-58c5-bf30-9a82b7757e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406660} {:answer/id #uuid "10ef096b-3da6-5469-9aaf-056abb9c9b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406663} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5e2bd6-42c5-529f-9f08-cbd53403ea48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406668} {:answer/id #uuid "b1384745-a3d2-57a2-83cf-2248f12bae46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406683} {:answer/id #uuid "c18a0f77-fbcd-5ceb-b30c-ca0b3feee4c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03292879-7e19-58c5-bf30-9a82b7757e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406660} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b902c9-1d56-5de9-9587-969c0111a4bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406695}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7faa34ab-beed-5e97-be56-8c5b15402d1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81739" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ec042b-d1ae-56d2-9c3b-5e465e3d4cd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256801} {:answer/id #uuid "1ceac5b9-64e6-5124-a6a6-85b5d1310188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257021} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd04f96-05c9-5672-a88f-0279e73ffdd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy+{x}^{2}z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257581} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f96662-a242-51e6-8a22-278c74c24b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258957} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc740e7-8445-5753-9183-33d9b76eb7e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+yz+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ceac5b9-64e6-5124-a6a6-85b5d1310188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257021} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3c753f6-334f-50c7-9748-b981b710da38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259320}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "16cbb2e0-e24c-5276-8cc2-e4e9a9ed6115" :question/math-academy-id "q-124729" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(2xz+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j+{x}^{2}\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xz+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2xz+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1602f189-0d28-56ae-9dd9-e128132e6ed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}+yz-{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256980} {:answer/id #uuid "56a824e1-89ad-5b9f-9d90-0a15c3c6afec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz-y{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257529} {:answer/id #uuid "83844a24-d36a-5bde-90cc-25704c4bd024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257944} {:answer/id #uuid "9676b23d-58f9-5906-86c8-bc56f352ddbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xz-{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258109} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfef173-d55d-5471-9bd3-2a9896819991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={y}^{3}z-x{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83844a24-d36a-5bde-90cc-25704c4bd024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257944} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf730883-1f0b-511a-85de-b890c410b87c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259364}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af3748dd-98e9-5ccb-8f93-b1501896f383" :question/math-academy-id "q-124733" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=({y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z})\\,i+2xy{z}^{3}\\,j+(3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z})\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy{z}^{3}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2xy{z}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z) & =3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z} \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61fd2894-92e2-5f21-a8ef-3e4ce0f75c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{x}^{2}-y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263843} {:answer/id #uuid "79e27da1-668f-5a0d-929e-edada25fd557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xy-y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264058} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4936ba-0c0b-5085-a53f-9463a50276eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xy+x{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264073} {:answer/id #uuid "ab37d3c3-0675-5158-a1cc-82765ce5104b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264470} {:answer/id #uuid "f52f7f29-86a2-537c-90f4-0eabb7389f49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xz-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab37d3c3-0675-5158-a1cc-82765ce5104b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264470} :answer-field/id #uuid "a355c26c-a760-5643-9305-ddb34ec96b30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265532}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "062ec003-5729-5fe0-9819-864cef26e212" :question/math-academy-id "q-132100" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1)\\,i+(\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2})\\,j-2yz\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =-{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=-y{z}^{2}+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2yz & =-2yz+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "005532ea-a569-5e6b-9518-a0150b3fb329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz{e}^{z}+x+y-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263006} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7694c1-e825-574d-a180-e04f18376fc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+x+{y}^{2}z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264082} {:answer/id #uuid "a49fbc3b-b203-5f1b-8f40-b0bcdbe9fbb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{z}+y{x}^{2}+x-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264402} {:answer/id #uuid "c04be1f6-81c5-54c2-ba54-5b752c684e10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+x+y-z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264673} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d7254b-5172-5dfd-9ea1-9404e617cc8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+y-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6d7254b-5172-5dfd-9ea1-9404e617cc8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+y-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264739} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9c4e81f-33ab-54ad-9dac-693d04696388" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265624}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6b7d90b3-0384-5231-a89f-b7683283ef28" :question/math-academy-id "q-132103" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(2x+y{e}^{z})\\,i+(x{e}^{z}+1)\\,j+(xy{e}^{z}-2z)\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}-2z\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2x+y{e}^{z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+xy{e}^{z}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x{e}^{z}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy{e}^{z}+y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}-2z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy{e}^{z}-2z & =xy{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =-2z \\\\ h(z) & =-{z}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+y-{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25284915-404f-5375-ac86-a55e513a4466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2yz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321435} {:answer/id #uuid "30d3adbe-dcd7-5a7c-b7f4-9d8234912198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321523} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae0e8e2-a744-52a6-8ad3-e46566c11f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321736} {:answer/id #uuid "acc3c980-cbdd-5610-8d47-878be5ead5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+yz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322501} {:answer/id #uuid "b7050df8-a7aa-52e2-bfb8-36dddf3c124d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{y}^{2}z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ae0e8e2-a744-52a6-8ad3-e46566c11f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321736} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0c9f1ab-635a-52c8-a0fd-14bac3805bc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323548}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f96b761e-8386-56f5-990d-c8300f6fbe05" :question/math-academy-id "q-36978" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,i+2yz\\,j+{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2yz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (2yz)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{2}z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)={y}^{2}z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z) & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351358 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16fb8477-c52e-58fb-b8cf-7472dfb09f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+xz+y+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348639} {:answer/id #uuid "1a6aa838-2967-5f6d-9ea6-bf9875d95e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz+xz+xy+yz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348670} {:answer/id #uuid "955bbc49-7db4-56b7-a02a-3e024df98843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+x+y+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349787} {:answer/id #uuid "9e6dc831-45cc-555d-ac7f-a066307d2999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz+x+y+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349861} {:answer/id #uuid "d71375c9-4e61-53ba-b6da-07758eef80e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+xz+x+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "955bbc49-7db4-56b7-a02a-3e024df98843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+x+y+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349787} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb7814d3-6540-596e-89a1-188d1c44755f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351031}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e18f3bc6-6740-5752-becc-b988af5a59c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-81740" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(y+1)\\,i+(x+z+1)\\,j+(y+1)\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+z+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (y+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =xy+x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+z+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x+z+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =z+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (z+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =yz+y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=yz+y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+x+yz+y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =y+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =1 \\\\ h(z) & =z+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+yz+x+y+z+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cca9c71-9e8d-570c-8946-ced4f1d50328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348697} {:answer/id #uuid "2a68e94a-4173-5ae7-858e-76a0731e97f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348821} {:answer/id #uuid "58860516-bdb9-5c40-81cc-99fbd0564951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=(x+y){e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349259} {:answer/id #uuid "5cca20bd-b007-5611-a5cf-cd625413e2d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{xy}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349300} {:answer/id #uuid "d106b7ad-431a-5269-9f98-9101b7fb0c7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xy}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a68e94a-4173-5ae7-858e-76a0731e97f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348821} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7a6d5b1-1736-5e32-bd6e-3958724c6738" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351115}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e91b9e21-1500-52f9-9378-332978dc9aa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-81741" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xy}\\,i+x{e}^{xy}\\,j+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{xy}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(z) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z) \\\\ h(z) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3z)+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2805a157-0ff6-5513-9530-54816d9d026a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2}+yz-2z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348790} {:answer/id #uuid "55830456-e7d8-5981-846c-7a4fe6003b50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=2xyz+y-2+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349229} {:answer/id #uuid "6146406d-3af2-5ae5-90e5-08d79844f945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz+yz-z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349337} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b8df1f-1d2d-5411-8b30-3ebb1730a80f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=2xyz+yz-2+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349887} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f652e8-b915-5303-b15e-d17266e3544c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=2xy{z}^{2}+y-z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2805a157-0ff6-5513-9530-54816d9d026a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2}+yz-2z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348790} :answer-field/id #uuid "35fcc368-86fe-5787-a4dc-f3208dbfc229" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350800}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "98c97ef5-5ea8-5162-8c84-a1645b2982e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-81751" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=y{z}^{2}\\,i+(x{z}^{2}+z)\\,j+(2xyz+y-2)\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{z}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{z}^{2}+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2xyz+y-2\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int y{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =xy{z}^{2}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{z}^{2}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{z}^{2}+z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{z}^{2}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x{z}^{2}+z \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =yz+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=yz+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2}+yz+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2xyz+y+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2xyz+y-2$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xyz+y-2 & =2xyz+y+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =-2 \\\\ h(z) & =-2z+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy{z}^{2}+yz-2z+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c1bf68-0a65-5287-b32f-c0e4dc3478f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xz{e}^{xz}+xz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348638} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1acbc8-d106-5b62-8d36-a7cb3a78a863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+yz+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350128} {:answer/id #uuid "d83bed69-94c4-50e6-be9a-7dcda2208ee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xz{e}^{xz}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350341} {:answer/id #uuid "f516143d-39f1-5b86-9e6a-3fe23efc5edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xy+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350590} {:answer/id #uuid "fda21360-2a77-5e5c-bdf6-2c33643d2238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fda21360-2a77-5e5c-bdf6-2c33643d2238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350678} :answer-field/id #uuid "b222692b-cb3d-5740-9bf6-1564da255bac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351013}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dfdbf33d-2f2f-5cc3-b43d-2537f87d3a7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81752" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=(yz{e}^{xz}+z)\\,i+({e}^{xz}+1)\\,j+(xy{e}^{xz}+x)\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=yz{e}^{xz}+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{xz}+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{xz}+x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (yz{e}^{xz}+z)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =y{e}^{xz}+xz+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{xz}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{xz}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xz}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & ={e}^{xz}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{xz}+x+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{xz}+x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy{e}^{xz}+x+{h}^{′}(z) & =zy{e}^{xz}+x \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}+xz+y+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd49044-48d7-5753-8938-92019f630b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+2y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406667} {:answer/id #uuid "4d50a1c5-e1f1-531f-8432-aafd884d17cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+2yz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406672} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2af6d4-f404-59ca-99d2-24985eb2eafa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406678} {:answer/id #uuid "a809662b-6dd9-5c73-b6ef-ca6fafbec01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz+y+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406681} {:answer/id #uuid "b668a6c8-35b7-56c2-aa49-6197e1a607d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+yz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f2af6d4-f404-59ca-99d2-24985eb2eafa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406678} :answer-field/id #uuid "419ffafb-e705-5c7d-80bf-60c8356135f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406691}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "327918f1-3cba-54fe-8e7b-c743d7b1d3cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-81755" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=2x{y}^{3}z\\,i+(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2})\\,j+({x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz)\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{3}z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2x{y}^{3}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int {z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=y{z}^{2}+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+y{z}^{2}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz+{h}^{′}(z) & ={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+y{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58749cbe-7816-57fd-a7c2-054a48490984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406675} {:answer/id #uuid "5d64b8a8-da33-5a82-92ab-d47162348e5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy-{y}^{2}+2yz+{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406676} {:answer/id #uuid "b90e506d-36bb-59dc-a9d8-181dd55c4171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+z{y}^{2}-y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406685} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d21bb7-6a0c-5042-8d17-4fbbdd6ece2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y-xy+x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406687} {:answer/id #uuid "ea19559e-ae30-58d1-a09e-446a3ce435cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}+yz-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea19559e-ae30-58d1-a09e-446a3ce435cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}+yz-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406689} :answer-field/id #uuid "a46e9509-621a-57db-9c8d-caf61cc840c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406693}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a894d767-b55b-50b3-ac91-79c05f30a84d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124731" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(2x+y)\\,i+(2y+x+z)\\,j+(y-2z)\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y+x+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y-2z\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+xy+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y+x+z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2y+x+z \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2y+z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (2y+z)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+yz+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)={y}^{2}+yz+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}+yz+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y-2z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2z & =y+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =-2z \\\\ h(z) & =-{z}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}+yz-{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124593 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124404 :question/id #uuid "c623bb85-401d-5d00-b4a8-cb86570d3304" :question/math-academy-id "e-8282" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}[f(\\mathbf r(t))]$ at $t = \\pi$ given that\n$\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x-1) \\, \\mathbf j$\nand\n$\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin{t} \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos{t} \\, \\mathbf j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x - 1) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x = \\sin{t}$ and $y = \\cos{t}$ into $\\nabla f(x,y)$ to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t = \\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(1)+0-(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124956 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05130439-f8ad-50ab-88fd-4aef2c512276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321194} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab62fb4-96f6-544e-a43f-7c54848d7b96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321475} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb77509-e2b1-57fe-aa81-c820e1759f9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321484} {:answer/id #uuid "dd29a8cb-f3e2-55a6-b61e-7dcb1a986936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322877} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8e8763-9fc2-54d7-beb9-a7f164362324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd29a8cb-f3e2-55a6-b61e-7dcb1a986936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322877} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0f23e9e-f039-5d42-b46f-a3ba271cfc87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323513}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ceb16fee-64a8-51ee-a3b7-7009e970259a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36215" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that\n$∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,j$\nand\n$r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(t)}{(t)^{2}+3({t}^{2})}\\,i+\\frac{3}{(t)^{2}+3({t}^{2})}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{4{t}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2t})\\cdot 1+(\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}})\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}+\\frac{3}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{2}{1}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15d810bf-905d-5d78-ba48-12ddc9b892e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357945} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6ad6c1-0c8d-5fb0-b435-dee857f73211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358135} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae9204f-c34d-510e-94bc-023fcf5f25fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358819} {:answer/id #uuid "9af214c2-c6da-5e82-8df8-28a9a5b98d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359108} {:answer/id #uuid "acbb10f0-bfb6-5636-9744-b6306c87e6ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9af214c2-c6da-5e82-8df8-28a9a5b98d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359108} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1da5512-5fc1-5bb7-8ab0-03b3b2b0ba70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360309}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72c15c65-cd14-5a6c-9b4a-a63cd2f705de" :question/math-academy-id "q-94872" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+t\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\frac{t}{2}$ and $y=t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))] & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "439aee45-933e-5525-9345-5b17029a63c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282266} {:answer/id #uuid "582ea5c1-1e57-5176-9167-972093895fd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282440} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb3e4fe-ec5b-5339-9652-4a5cb0a96a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282782} {:answer/id #uuid "848c5f97-904d-5825-a1c2-c3a1c330760f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282831} {:answer/id #uuid "e9802ab6-19b7-5148-b0e2-623781519ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "848c5f97-904d-5825-a1c2-c3a1c330760f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282831} :answer-field/id #uuid "353e1da0-d350-56e5-9994-3f64a6bca475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284029}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2df41f1-db14-5e76-bbfb-6cc0c8cd31a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-167497" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\pi $ given that $∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=\\frac{t}{2}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j) \\\\  & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\pi ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (-1)+0}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29453b2c-6d17-5788-a24a-a3018b5c97bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282046} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1dd875-1ab5-5d5c-8cee-d4a760e0fad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282077} {:answer/id #uuid "36e21432-61e2-5f75-bf7b-6c17d3bac35a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282176} {:answer/id #uuid "878e3c0b-0fa7-530a-8b50-3b0123c90209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282857} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ea2448-8707-56af-883d-3fdfe6fbad5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29453b2c-6d17-5788-a24a-a3018b5c97bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282046} :answer-field/id #uuid "99251eef-a601-5418-8667-74393704a93e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284194}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15c14f08-def7-5eb6-8ced-3b42bca65403" :question/math-academy-id "q-167498" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=-1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+(t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=t+{cos}^{2}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡t+t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡t+t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t+1}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t+1}\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t+1}\\cdot (1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t+1}-2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(-1))] & ={e}^{-1+1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b203b6e-7830-50da-aa18-f454f862a948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321238} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf6d8fc-e1b8-5fdc-b9b5-09f8f6941c20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321497} {:answer/id #uuid "c175b73f-e2fb-54e8-a7e0-b2906ff65bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322651} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d08b91-916d-5adb-a62d-964ea2cc4e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi +2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322710} {:answer/id #uuid "e1542b36-0e72-5814-8c1f-4765946bc3e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi +1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cf6d8fc-e1b8-5fdc-b9b5-09f8f6941c20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321497} :answer-field/id #uuid "276fef95-8127-5751-a8f3-63d27774306c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323248}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdba027b-12bc-5b22-aa20-f040a6af0b36" :question/math-academy-id "q-37106" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j$ and $r(t)=(2{t}^{2}+2)\\,i+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=2{t}^{2}+2$ and $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =4t\\,i+\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (4t\\,i+\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot 4t+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t+1}{({t}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{2(1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1+1}{({1}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+1)}{(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323883 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c36ce21-00fb-5fff-a854-09a22fe9b12a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321487} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8cc371-8d3d-50ee-84ac-7aa30e752029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321639} {:answer/id #uuid "51cf7225-599b-5037-bcde-98e28ee76a46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321791} {:answer/id #uuid "842b9eff-5030-5ffd-9f05-2ac210a4ff66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322191} {:answer/id #uuid "f84babd5-90be-52b2-8398-56d622fd27c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f84babd5-90be-52b2-8398-56d622fd27c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323099} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b9f0d4f-747d-5dbd-9a61-61b8b32ac61a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323349}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48ca6f9a-7d23-5701-9123-39d54477cf36" :question/math-academy-id "q-37109" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=0$ given that $∇f(x,y)=y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\,i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,j$ and $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\,i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x={e}^{2t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2t}+1)\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(2t+1)\\,i+2t{e}^{2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(2t+1)\\,i+2t{e}^{2t}\\,j)\\cdot (2{e}^{2t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(2t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+4t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}[(2t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =2{e}^{2(0)}((2(0)+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0))+2(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(0)))=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "336ae663-8acc-530e-96cc-0c9939efc8b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358206} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8323a2-f87f-54c1-ba41-37494660f68d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e+{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358429} {:answer/id #uuid "68b9473e-f106-5375-ac81-7275f6b0a9eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-2{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358670} {:answer/id #uuid "734c0594-8b9d-5fed-bb61-015770db6c72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358754} {:answer/id #uuid "f34de6af-084c-5ec3-8d75-77cbd5aa09e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68b9473e-f106-5375-ac81-7275f6b0a9eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-2{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358670} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e56fa10-b537-572f-8386-98aeb21d532f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360205}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97fd3ba8-8ab5-5d07-adaa-98357dd8d4e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94873" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=2x\\,i+2y\\,j$ and $r(t)={e}^{-t}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2x\\,i+2y\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x={e}^{-t}$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2{e}^{-t}\\,i+2{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-t})\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{-t}\\,i+2{e}^{t}\\,j)\\cdot ((-{e}^{-t})\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})+2{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =2{e}^{2(1)}-2{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}-2{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2963cff8-a340-5205-a3e9-f39819e56fcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358130} {:answer/id #uuid "4b15c439-f511-541c-b00a-276ff973830a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358394} {:answer/id #uuid "78ff0f13-6c5c-58bf-8edf-2283322de497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358802} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b339d0-3302-5c8a-b8b0-29fc07054890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359154} {:answer/id #uuid "f3204c09-87cb-5a4b-9803-80dcb1e84a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2963cff8-a340-5205-a3e9-f39819e56fcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358130} :answer-field/id #uuid "a36cec20-678c-5599-8ac5-1c5bef7d549d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360277}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3d7bbdd-6122-519c-8393-02c2402f7ab7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94878" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,i-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j$ and $r(t)=2\\sqrt{t}\\,i-2\\sqrt{t}\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,i-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=2\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=-2\\sqrt{t}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =-(-2\\sqrt{t})\\operatorname{sin}⁡[(2\\sqrt{t})(-2\\sqrt{t})]\\,i-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡[(2\\sqrt{t})(-2\\sqrt{t})]\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2\\sqrt{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}})j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}})i) \\\\  & =(-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}+2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{8}))] & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124405 :question/id #uuid "4940fa22-1584-565f-81fe-dc12e5132f23" :question/math-academy-id "e-8294" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac12x^2+y^2,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125039 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db88b087-b136-58ed-9ecd-7896e0822fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r^{2}\\cos (2θ) + r^{2}\\sin (2θ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122835} {:answer/id #uuid "f39a24b5-23ab-5f9d-bfe3-258ca717c94f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3r^{2}\\cos (2θ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122836} {:answer/id #uuid "2a439bde-7060-5f47-907a-df53e7c751d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\sin (2θ) + 2r\\sin^{2} θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122837} {:answer/id #uuid "2edc2883-3d8d-53a0-bb5d-d106db10244f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\cos (2θ) + r\\sin (2θ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122838} {:answer/id #uuid "74f961ba-683e-50da-8c1a-e63f2d0ae676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\cos θ + r^{2}\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122839}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a86a93b6-feda-5a79-b8d8-74c4acc61388" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122834} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79830e35-fb58-5c0c-8ffe-3455034ae32b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368497} {:answer/id #uuid "e18a672b-de77-5df8-8f1b-84076247b048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368498} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7be39b-6734-5fbb-b31d-4ee6cfc206ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368499} {:answer/id #uuid "633b9946-97ef-5a0d-a217-10b115c3dbb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368500} {:answer/id #uuid "1cddd4cb-d4e0-5346-8409-af4fe2ea10fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1cddd4cb-d4e0-5346-8409-af4fe2ea10fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368501} :answer-field/id #uuid "77fe4fb6-8827-5540-a442-722c8b1aa5b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368496}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0722c4ed-9b45-5a82-9aca-63ef2538a12b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37192" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=2xy+{y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6f0a484-c1df-59cd-8a67-536b8a6b6fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122841} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d87c61-ce4c-51b4-a48d-e8a1bd1e34a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122842} {:answer/id #uuid "e8df0f85-5951-5810-997c-6d9f9ec513ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos θ - \\sin θ}{\\cos θ + \\sin θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122843} {:answer/id #uuid "29657135-0af4-5058-ad50-f0440efad395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{r\\cos θ + r\\sin θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122844} {:answer/id #uuid "2bad1c34-a8db-52f0-b044-93c8515a9174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122845}] :answer-field/id #uuid "086dfdbb-92b2-5cae-9238-3f1a3c6feab0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122840}] :question/id #uuid "08b52ee2-9e81-5a5f-aeda-8fc812032d41" :question/math-academy-id "q-37196" :question/problem "Given that $g(x, y) = \\ln (x + y)$, find $\\frac{∂g}{∂r}$, where $(x, y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r, θ)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7120b822-0ff6-547d-b7c2-2241a49aca6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358738} {:answer/id #uuid "7d848135-2b15-5316-9a46-e4e3b36ec08b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358845} {:answer/id #uuid "811e3aba-e8b3-5975-bf14-431f1c3fe93e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358879} {:answer/id #uuid "9029ca1e-4b6a-555a-8f91-25f0d427d82f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359010} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b0a061-fad6-5c22-b008-8d6182556cb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9029ca1e-4b6a-555a-8f91-25f0d427d82f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359010} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d76e27a-bad6-5130-9b46-77d20a6d4839" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360203}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4970bf30-0b07-5dcd-b3de-478f21daf6db" :question/math-academy-id "q-94999" :question/problem "Given that $h(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $h(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & ={r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r} & =(2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360563 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dce7e8f-7060-53e3-85cb-4987af63f4e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358164} {:answer/id #uuid "6202b197-e3e5-5891-8722-848e41ddd428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{r}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358606} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc5a7fb-705f-5c74-b33e-9a9c4a94bf6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358965} {:answer/id #uuid "e6251725-fa38-5c2d-a0b0-c05928f16370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359789} {:answer/id #uuid "e704bf2b-93ca-5b8f-8349-92ea61dd3fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6251725-fa38-5c2d-a0b0-c05928f16370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359789} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cbcd748-fe7e-5cef-acf6-5edffcf9bb9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360136}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3f6701f-c993-5ff2-926d-7219617af2de" :question/math-academy-id "q-95109" :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =\\frac{2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =(\\frac{2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2r({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360564 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c5cf6f4-8993-5a59-935b-fbd20502b0fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358151} {:answer/id #uuid "51d099de-8e46-57d2-b3a9-1bf43d6107d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358456} {:answer/id #uuid "5c370ba9-4b6a-5155-8e84-b69d91cecfd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358553} {:answer/id #uuid "6b781cf2-8fa4-56d4-825d-5429ed24f588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358695} {:answer/id #uuid "abad9f8c-0b50-5c33-9de5-9335756322a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c5cf6f4-8993-5a59-935b-fbd20502b0fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358151} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5464eca-9902-5070-94ce-68bcd1dd8713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360403}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bc0127e-5521-50db-8fdb-586719893922" :question/math-academy-id "q-95112" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +0\\,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "255b1439-4503-5ba1-9467-b4cbf23cf837" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358086} {:answer/id #uuid "2826740b-006a-5fb8-af1b-b04cb4e430c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358112} {:answer/id #uuid "8f20dc0c-2f9c-59cb-9371-1e2bab94de58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358999} {:answer/id #uuid "c17a4d57-ab7f-5cad-9c18-15918d10a4db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359449} {:answer/id #uuid "ff52e99a-a36a-570b-927e-6775bb16f2f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff52e99a-a36a-570b-927e-6775bb16f2f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359997} :answer-field/id #uuid "d14ca8c8-8191-546e-952e-035f83d5d2de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360352}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a5ea0e8-c2a8-56bd-bc0a-1183f811a786" :question/math-academy-id "q-95113" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)={y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂g}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta } & =0\\,(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fe46cca-410f-5c55-88ef-8d80e7031ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358726} {:answer/id #uuid "ba7b642c-5011-5fe6-b8b3-8ba15e13f44b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359374} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc682ef-5b2d-5c3c-8130-e2f1ce1ee474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359430} {:answer/id #uuid "e32325f6-ab86-53be-a9ca-0e5883831318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359761} {:answer/id #uuid "fb9162b2-a7fc-5e49-88a2-4b87f9188d83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfc682ef-5b2d-5c3c-8130-e2f1ce1ee474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359430} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ebf0a96-067c-5319-889e-a652fa3fcdea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360139}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09b6c584-862f-5f2c-8d59-a960b719bfdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95116" :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂w}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=x,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124595 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124406 :question/id #uuid "a0246ee6-702f-5d8d-8482-8db1c46b32ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-8295" :question/problem "Given that $w(r, \\theta)=r \\cos 3\\theta,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial r}\\cos\\theta - \\dfrac{1}{r}\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }=-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "879d